The so-called 'asperand' symbol '@', or 'commercial at', is even in 
itself a graphical representation of the letters 'a' and 't' written 
together. Just like '&' for 'e' and 't'. (Some typefaces even have the 
t-dash showing at the end of the 'tail'.)
'@' *is* 'at', just written another way. The symbol was used as a 
shorthand when writing things like 'amount@price-per-unit', 
'person@place' or 'place@time' etc long before the advent of email, but 
seldom seen outside the English language. Hence, many non-native (and 
especially the young) English speakers has not seen the symbol in 
another context, and only see it as a cryptic symbol.


